\begin{document}

\title{AI Inference Monitoring for a PCB AOI Workstation:\\
System Design, Case Study, and Evaluation}

\author{
\IEEEauthorblockN{Lys}
\IEEEauthorblockA{
\textit{Thesis Draft} \\
\textit{Automatic Optical Inspection Monitoring Study} \\
University / Department Placeholder \\
Email: author@example.com
}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This thesis presents a monitoring-oriented architecture for AI inference services
in an industrial Automatic Optical Inspection (AOI) workflow for printed circuit
board (PCB) review. The work focuses on operational visibility rather than only
model accuracy. A repository-aligned case study is used in which a FastAPI-based
AOI backend produces structured inference events, a React workstation supports
human review, and a Loki--Promtail--Grafana stack captures and visualizes system
behavior. The thesis is organized into six chapters covering motivation,
related work, system design, implementation, experiments, and evaluation. The
proposed design emphasizes structured JSON logs, defect-level traceability,
latency observation, and anomaly detection for normal, failure, and degraded
inference conditions. The resulting document provides a practical thesis
foundation for demonstrating how an AI service can be monitored as an
operational system instead of being treated as an isolated model artifact.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
AI monitoring, AOI, PCB inspection, inference logging, Loki, Grafana, Promtail,
FastAPI, observability, industrial AI
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Chapter 1: Introduction}

Artificial intelligence models are often evaluated through offline metrics such
as accuracy, precision, recall, or mAP. While those metrics are necessary, they
are not sufficient once the model becomes part of a running system. In a real
inspection line, operators and engineers need to know not only whether a model
can produce predictions, but also whether the service remains responsive,
whether failures are traceable, and whether abnormal behavior can be detected
quickly enough to prevent operational disruption.

This requirement is especially important in AI-assisted AOI systems for PCB
inspection. AOI services interact with image acquisition, run management,
defect review, and operator decisions. A defect result without metadata about
timestamp, board identity, latency, confidence, or review outcome provides only
partial value. Industrial use requires inference to be observable as a process.

The central thesis of this document is that inference monitoring must be
designed as a first-class system capability. Instead of adding logs as an
afterthought, the system should generate structured events that describe what
happened, when it happened, how long it took, and what downstream consequence it
produced. This makes the AI pipeline auditable and easier to debug, benchmark,
and improve.

The study uses a repository-aligned AOI workstation as the case environment.
The software stack includes a FastAPI backend, a React review interface, local
storage, JSONL event logging, and a Docker-based observability stack with Loki,
Promtail, and Grafana. The monitoring goal is to detect three broad operating
states: normal behavior, explicit failure behavior, and degraded high-latency
behavior.

The objectives of the thesis are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item explain why inference monitoring matters in operational AI systems;
    \item review observability practices relevant to ML services;
    \item design a monitoring architecture suitable for a local AOI platform;
    \item describe a concrete implementation grounded in the PCB AOI project;
    \item propose experiments that simulate multiple runtime conditions; and
    \item evaluate whether the system supports practical anomaly detection.
\end{itemize}

