\section{Chapter 5: Experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Goal}
The experimental objective is to determine whether the monitoring stack can
differentiate between normal and abnormal inference behavior. Because the
project already supports mock event generation, experiments can be staged in a
controlled way without depending on a finished production detector.

\subsection{Scenario 1: Normal Operation}
In the normal scenario, the system emits a steady stream of events with a mix of
\texttt{PASS} and \texttt{FAIL} outcomes and stable latency values. The expected
dashboard result is a consistent event count, predictable failure frequency, and
latency within a narrow baseline range.

\subsection{Scenario 2: Error-Dominant Operation}
In the error scenario, the event stream is altered to produce an abnormally high
rate of failure outcomes or malformed service behavior. The key observation is
whether Grafana panels make the shift immediately visible through a spike in
\texttt{FAIL} events and whether log queries support rapid investigation of the
underlying records.

\subsection{Scenario 3: High-Latency Operation}
In the latency-degradation scenario, inference latency is artificially increased
while the rest of the event schema remains valid. This tests whether the system
can detect quality-of-service degradation even when predictions are still being
generated. In practical deployments, this scenario is important because slow
models can disrupt inspection throughput without producing explicit exceptions.

\subsection{Measurements}
The proposed measurements are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item event ingestion count over fixed windows;
    \item fail-event count over fixed windows;
    \item number of distinct boards observed;
    \item per-event inference latency;
    \item dashboard visibility of each scenario; and
    \item query responsiveness for recent log inspection.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Planned Experimental Scenarios}
\label{tab:experiments}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.20\columnwidth} p{0.23\columnwidth} p{0.42\columnwidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Scenario} & \textbf{Injected Condition} & \textbf{Expected Monitoring Signal} \\
\midrule
Normal run & Stable mixed events & Balanced event traffic and baseline latency \\
Error run & Elevated fail outputs & Visible increase in fail counts and suspicious logs \\
High-latency run & Slower inference times & Latency panels rise while event traffic continues \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Expected Outcome}
If the monitoring design is effective, each scenario should produce a distinct
visual and queryable signature. The normal case establishes the baseline, the
error case highlights defect-heavy or unstable behavior, and the latency case
demonstrates that performance degradation can be detected separately from logic
failure.

